Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Semi-fusing strings", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977300719364835*^9, 
  3.977300787458941*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"346690fa-7567-4b11-bbe4-\
fd0bb21a583f"],

Cell["\<\
(all strings except the maximal string is fused; mode numbers are chosen to \
be maximal with the standard way of constructing rigged configurations)\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977300788523776*^9, 
  3.9773007923115683`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d8b678d9-e26c-4439-abae-\
376e070d130b"],

Cell["\<\
NOTES on functions: 
1. SolutionFromModeConfiguration[modes,length] is the function I call to find \
string solutions (in StringSolver). From this function, sol[1] is a list of \
centres of strings and is the object I use.

2. log[x,branch choice, branch cut] is listed in StringSolver

3. Note that I take v[a,s,k]->-v[a,s,k] before putting in numbers from sol[1] \
from StringSolver. This is due to StringSolver and WronskianSolver using \
different conventions.

4. SolutionSYTW[SYT] is the function I call from WronskianSolver to get exact \
solutions as a list of c coefficients (I store as exactsol=SolutionSYTW[SYT] \
where exactsol[[-1,-1]] is the final list of c coefficients; \
exactsol[[-2,-1]] is the second final; and so on).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977301247016425*^9, 3.9773013220709047`*^9}, {
  3.9773014692127047`*^9, 3.977301479412788*^9}, {3.977301617503426*^9, 
  3.977301665044125*^9}, {3.9773018688163033`*^9, 
  3.9773019436915007`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8527528b-4d33-4846-a98c-\
334bfc809a69"],

